  "N16" [label="(11,1,14) Ref opinion: Transcript levels alone are insufficient to predict protein levels or explain genotype-phenotype relationships"];
  "N7" [label="(7,0,0) Existing models don't capture full AD spectrum and show less severe neurodegeneration than humans"];
  "N9" [label="(9,0,0) Omics technologies enable thorough evaluation of disease models and comparison with human data"];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study aims to perform deep, age-resolved proteomic and phosphoproteomic profiling of multiple Alzheimer’s disease mouse models, compare the mouse molecular profiles with human AD data, and measure protein turnover to investigate shared and model-specific molecular pathways, including whether delayed degradation of amyloid-associated proteins helps explain transcript–protein discrepancies and model relevance to human AD.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) The retained and repaired terminal conclusions jointly motivate the paper-level research proposal."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Because existing Alzheimer’s disease mouse models do not capture the full human AD spectrum and show limitations such as less severe neurodegeneration, improved characterization of these models is needed."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Proteomic profiling may provide complementary information beyond transcriptomic analyses because proteomics can reveal protein changes not captured by transcript levels alone."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) The limitations of early AD proteomic studies in depth, model scope, and human comparison motivate a more comprehensive deep proteomic analysis."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Protein degradation mechanisms may contribute to transcript–protein discrepancies, because degradation rates can affect steady-state protein levels independently of synthesis."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Deep proteomic profiling across Alzheimer’s disease mouse models with human comparison is motivated as a way to improve model evaluation and address prior limits in proteomic depth, model scope, and human comparison."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) A suitable research approach is to develop and evaluate deep proteomic and phosphoproteomic profiling of Alzheimer’s disease mouse models with human–mouse comparison and protein-turnover measurement to investigate transcript–protein discrepancies and model-relevant molecular pathways."];
